concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23xi a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "23XI General rules for carrying out forensic procedures"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-23xi ;
    values:originalText "A forensic procedure:  (a) must be carried out in circumstances affording reasonable privacy to the suspect; and  (b) except as permitted (expressly or impliedly) by other provisions of this Part, must not be carried out in the presence or view of a person who is of the opposite sex to the suspect; and  (c) must not be carried out in the presence or view of a person whose presence is not necessary for the purposes of the forensic procedure or required or permitted by another provision of this Part; and  (d) must not involve the removal of more clothing than is necessary for the carrying out of the procedure; and  (e) must not involve more visual inspection than is necessary for the carrying out of the procedure. 23XIA  No questioning during forensic procedure  A forensic procedure must not be carried out while the suspect is being questioned as defined in subsection 23B(6). If questioning has not been completed before the forensic procedure is to be carried out, it must be suspended while the forensic procedure is carried out. 23XIB  Suspect must be cautioned before forensic procedure starts  Before anyone starts to carry out a forensic procedure on a suspect, a constable must caution the suspect that he or she does not have to say anything while the procedure is carried out but that anything the person does say may be used in evidence.   Forensic procedures  Part ID Carrying out forensic procedures on suspects  Division 6  Section 23XJ  Crimes Act 1914  Compilation date: 04/06/2026" ;
    skos:definition "A forensic procedure:  (a) must be carried out in circumstances affording reasonable privacy to the suspect; and  (b) except as permitted (expressly or impliedly) by other provisions of this Part, must not be carried out in the presence or view of a person who is of the opposite sex to the suspect; and  (c) must not be carried out in the presence or view of a person whose presence is not necessary for the purposes of the forensic procedure or required or permitted by another provision of this Part; and  (d) must not involve the removal of more clothing than is necessary for the carrying out of the procedure; and  (e) must not involve more visual inspection than is necessary for the carrying out of the procedure. 23XIA  No questioning during forensic procedure  A forensic procedure must not be carried out while the suspect is being questioned as defined in subsection 23B(6). If questioning has not been completed before the forensic procedure is to be carried out, it must be suspended while the forensic procedure is carried out. 23XIB  Suspect must be cautioned before forensic procedure starts  Before anyone starts to carry out a forensic procedure on a suspect, a constable must caution the suspect that he or she does not have to say anything while the procedure is carried out but that anything the person does say may be used in evidence.   Forensic procedures  Part ID Carrying out forensic procedures on suspects  Division 6  Section 23XJ  Crimes Act 1914  Compilation date: 04/06/2026"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "5dd10dd69ae977e635a50086a30f775f76fdd899f6ca11c0c156e9b3b91edba8" ;
    cof:pageNumber "62"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:note "Forensic procedures must be carried out under certain conditions." ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
